## C1 — The production detection model is θ-deterministic (latent-thresholded), not data-thresholded. [LOCAL, verified in source]

`simulation_detection_probability.py:169-182`:

> For a source with measured optimal SNR_raw at luminosity distance `d_L_k`, the
> **detection horizon** is the distance at which it would sit exactly at
> threshold: `d_hor_k = SNR_raw_k * d_L_k / snr_threshold` [Gpc].
> **Detection is deterministic (SNR >= threshold ⇔ d_L <= d_hor)**, so the
> detection probability is the survival function of the horizon distribution:
> `p_det(d_L) = P(d_hor >= d_L)`. The horizon set is independent of h …

`SNR_raw` is the **optimal (noiseless) SNR at the injection's true parameters**;
the cut is therefore a deterministic function of θ, not of the noisy data d.
Independently corroborated by `pp_coverage_pdetnum_20260711/SUMMARY.md:90-91`:
*"production also thresholds SNR on the noiseless injected waveform
(latent-thresholded class)"*.

**Consequence (MFG 2019 Eqs. 5–7):** for latent thresholding the indicator
`1[det(θ)]` sits **inside** the numerator's θ-integral and only leaves it if every
coordinate it depends on is retained as data. The MFG "no p_det in the numerator"
form is the *data-thresholded* special case.

**Refute by:** show that `SNR_raw` in the injection CSVs is a noise-realised
(matched-filter) SNR rather than the optimal SNR of the injected waveform, or that
the analysed CRB catalogue was thresholded on a noisy statistic. Either makes
detection data-deterministic and C1 fails, taking C3–C6 with it.

